{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgB1HwRKa94atyq18KLN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote the candidate so it fits well within the time limit, but it is likely to be pruned: its ELPD-LOO is about −4400, roughly 965 nats behind the best model (−3435.5).\n\n**What changed.** The first attempt (`indifference_band_tally_switch`) put the indifference band on top of the full person-level model: four sets of 400 per-person parameters, plus a band width that traded off against each person's sensitivity. It was still sampling at the 30-minute limit. The repair is `shared_indifference_band_tally_switch`:\n- **Same idea:** the choice rule has a smooth, shared indifference band. When the two sequences differ only slightly in how random they feel, people pick close to a coin flip; only the part of the difference beyond the band pushes them to one side. This is meant to explain the critique's (weak, q = 0.29) finding that people's choice rates per pair are more polarised than the best model predicts.\n- **Simpler evidence:** as the brief asks, everyone shares the same inputs — how close a sequence's heads-minus-tails tally span is to an expected span, and how close its switching rate is to an ideal rate. There are no per-person parameters and no left/right lean, so the model has 5 free parameters. Features are computed once per pair and the likelihood is vectorised.\n- **Span term rewritten:** an earlier version with an \"expected span\" parameter had 3 divergent transitions in 2000 draws, above the 0.1% admission limit, because the expected span and its weight traded off. I rewrote that term as two linear coefficients, which is mathematically the same family.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Admission self-check:** `OK`.\n- **My own full fit** (4 chains × 1000 draws, single core): 0 divergent transitions, R-hat 1.00, bulk ESS ≥ 650, about 8.5 minutes in total. It should be faster in admission if the chains run